\f1\fs22 Then he focussed on the VorpalTech logo, reducing it to fragments the size of defective corn flakes that they always put at the bottom of the bag to pad the weight.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 His beady, koala eyes misted over, and he made a meal of ripping up the next card.\
At least now he had real work to do: rescuing Sarah from the clutches of his evil stepmother. Though Eloise was, in fact, his biological mater, it helped fuel his sense of mission to think of her as diabolical spell-casting witch who had ruined his life.\
Her, and her accomplices, all those incidentally cast members she had sullied his narrative with, mere production assistants to her show-runner and executive producer credits. That woman Hayley what\'92s-her-name, who Alexander realised was probably trying to get her own show right from the beginning. The hippy woman who had a lab coat fetish and was clearly not a doctor, though Alexander found himself attracted to her model-next-door looks and her commitment to cosplay. Tax, maybe even Chanta was in on it, after decades of propping up his cleaning business by throwing crime scenes his way. Chanta had probably gone broke in his absence.\
They were all conspiring to keep him from Sarah, when she needed him more than ever. She\'92d probably had their baby months ago, had stopped being fat, and needed him more than ever. They\'92d been denied a husband and a father. Alexander knew he\'92d make a great father; he\'92d been working on treatments for the role, and his whole father arc, since he\'92d returned from exile nearly three weeks earlier.\
\'93Quantum Reality Lenses, my arse\'85 my perfectly well-sculpted butt.\'94 Alexander stopped ripping up business cards to workshop his line. \'93Quantum Reality Lenses, my tax returns\'85 my\'85?\'94\
The cards still needed tearing up.\
Alexander was still torn up about believing there really were two-headed cows and American cheese that tasted like that French stuff they matured in stolen socks.\
\'93We have sound from the restaurant, sir,\'94 a voice came through his ear-piece.\
\'93Good. Just give me a minute.\'94 Alexander was determined to destroy every last VorpalTech card that had ever been printed. Still, these Sages\'97the seven minus four sages, that just seem to pop in and out of scenes without warning\'85\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\i \cf2 I mean, who writes a plot like that?
\f1\i0  Alexander thought. 
\f3\i I mean
\f1\i0 , 
\f3\i what\'92s their motivation?
\f1\i0  
\f3\i What makes me care about them?\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Initially, Alexander had written off the wizened old jugglers as nothing more than comic relief. But it seemed they\'92d been residing in one of the guest townhouses at Sublime Point since before his mother had exiled him to a cheese country. The Sages came and went as they pleased. By all accounts, they had some interest in the side project his mother had tempted Sarah with.\
Alexander realised their complete inexplicability is what made them so suspicious. That, and the fact that the camera hated them\'97it was impossible to get a decent close-up without making them look naked except for their beards. Whatever plot arcs they were involved in, they were clearly up to their ridiculous, pointed caps in it.\
Sooner or later they\'92d give up their guilt and Alexander could write up the chapter in his memoirs. He tore up his last business card. \
\'93Have we got visual?\'94 Alexander spoke into his lapel mic.\
\'93No.\'94\
\'93Why not?\'94\
\'93Restaurant requires a booking for at least ten for parties and undercover operations.\'94\
\'93Well didn\'92t we make a booking?\'94 Alexander asked and was already picturing the producer he would fire.\
\'93We tried, but they wanted a hundred-and-fifty percent deposit.\'94\
\'93On what?\'94\
\'93Average surveillance operation takings, including a complimentary gift bag of tzatziki, retsina, and baklava.\'94\
\'93How can they bill us for complimentary gift bags?\'94 Alexander hated when locations tried to shake down production crews.\
\'93They\'92re not billing us, it\'92s a deposit.\'94\
\'93Tax.\'94\
\'93Non-refundable,\'94 the voice in Alexander\'92s ear said.\
\'93What the tax? They bill us another fifty percent over what we spend, even if we order everything they expect us to?\'94\
The voice in the ear got impatient. \'93See this is why I hate location logistics. I guess they just expect you to order up to the deposit you\'92ve already paid.\'94\
Even Alexander could do the arithmetic on that. \'93But then, 
\f3\i that 
\f1\i0 becomes the new average which they use for the next hundred-and-fifty-percent rort.\'94\
\'93They say couples are more profitable than parties. They have to make up the difference somehow. I think they just hate parties.\'94\
\'93And legitimate investigative journalism,\'94 Alexander added.\
\'93They called us spies. Apparently production crews drink the cheapest piss and eat all the bar olives.\'94\
Alexander shook his head. \'93So, just sound then?\'94\
\'93What?\'94\
\'93Very funny. Okay, patch me in.\'94\
After thirty seconds, Alexander complained, \'93Jesus, that all just sounds like Greek to me.\'94\
\'93Jesus was Roman. But yes, they\'92re speaking Greek.\'94\
\'93Well, can you patch the translation software in?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93Good. Then fire yourself.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

